IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT domain
IMPORT `g7-interpretation`

-- g7-art37-43-final: Part 9, Articles 37 to 43: offences by corporate and other bodies (37), service of notices (38), Regulations and Orders (39), savings and transitional provisions (40), the enabling powers of the
-- States over other enactments (41, 42) and citation (43).
--
-- Article 37 is the one rule here that decides an outcome about people: when an officer of a body that offended is also guilty, and of what penalty.  It reaches a limited liability partnership, a separate limited
-- partnership or a body corporate, and the partners and officers of those (trap T26); an unincorporated association and the trustees of a trust are NOT named (fork F7.6 carries the other reading).  Whether it is
-- “proved” that the offence was committed with the consent or connivance of the person is a finding the caller supplies.
-- Articles 38 to 42 are powers of the Minister or the States, so each is a regulative MAY rule typed by who acts and what is done; the content of the Order or Regulations is an input.  Where the text says “as appear
-- to the States to be necessary or expedient” the rule takes that opinion as an input (it is a discretion).  Articles 41 and 42 are enabling powers to make Regulations that amend OTHER enactments (ruling 4 of the
-- brief): the power is encoded; the enactments themselves and the Regulations are not in the sources.  Article 43 is inert.  Article 40(1) is a rule of construction and is carried inert.

§ `Part 9 — Miscellaneous and Final Provisions`

§§ `The powers exercised under Articles 38 to 42`

-- What is done under these Articles, by what is done.
DECLARE `Power exercised under Articles 38 to 42` IS ONE OF
    `make an Order making further provision as to the service of a notice`
    `include transitional, consequential, incidental, supplementary or savings provisions in an Order`
    `include transitional, consequential, incidental, supplementary or savings provisions in Regulations`
    `make Regulations whose amendment of a provision of this Law includes consequential amendments of other provisions of it`
    `make Regulations making other saving provision`
    `make Regulations making transitional provision for bringing this Law into force`
    `make Regulations amending a relevant enactment to enable disclosure of information to the Commissioner`
    `make Regulations amending the Trusts (Jersey) Law 1984 and the Foundations (Jersey) Law 2009 to apply the charity test or the definition of charitable purposes`
    `make Regulations amending another enactment, not this Law, for consequential provision on references to charities`

-- src:727 | 37 Offences by corporate and other bodies
§§ `Article 37 — Offences by corporate and other bodies`

-- The kinds of body that can offend, as Article 37(1) and Article 2(1) name them, and the two the Article does not name but Article 2(1) makes entities (fork F7.6).
DECLARE `Kind of body that offended under Article 37` IS ONE OF
    `a limited liability partnership`
    `a separate limited partnership`
    `a body corporate`
    `an unincorporated association`
    `the trustees of a trust`

-- The capacity of a person in relation to the body, as sub-paragraph (a) lists it.  The member of a body corporate managed by its members is added by Article 37(2).
DECLARE `Capacity of a person in relation to a body under Article 37` IS ONE OF
    `a partner of the partnership`
    `a director of the body corporate`
    `a governor of the body corporate`
    `a secretary of the body corporate`
    `another officer similar to a director, governor or secretary of the body corporate`
    `a member of a body corporate whose affairs are managed by its members, in connection with the member’s functions of management`
    `none of these`

-- Sub-paragraphs (a) and (b): the person holds the capacity, or only purports to act in it.
DECLARE `How a person comes within a capacity under Article 37` IS ONE OF
    `holds the capacity`
    `purports to act in the capacity`

-- The facts Article 37 asks about.
DECLARE `Facts for Article 37` HAS
    `kind of body` IS A `Kind of body that offended under Article 37` @desc The kind of body that committed the offence
    `the body committed an offence under this Law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an offence under this Law was committed by the body
    `consent or connivance is proved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is proved that the offence was committed with the consent or connivance of the person (a finding the caller supplies)
    capacity IS A `Capacity of a person in relation to a body under Article 37` @desc The capacity in which the person is, or purports to act, in relation to the body
    `how the person comes within the capacity` IS A `How a person comes within a capacity under Article 37` @desc Whether the person holds the capacity or only purports to act in it

-- 37(2) Where the affairs of a body corporate are managed by its members, paragraph (1) applies in relation to acts and defaults of a member in connection with the member’s functions of management as if the member
-- were a director of the body corporate.
-- src:732 | (2) Where the affairs of a body corporate are managed by its members, paragraph (1) applies in relation to acts and defaults of a member in connection with the member’s functions of management as if the member were a director of the body corporate.
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 37(2) (src:732)
@export Whether a member of a body corporate whose affairs are managed by its members is treated as a director for the purposes of Article 37(1) of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014: the body is a body corporate managed by its members and the act or default is in connection with the member’s functions of management
GIVEN facts IS A `Facts for Article 37` @desc The facts Article 37 asks about
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 37(2) the member is treated as a director of the body corporate` facts MEANS
        facts's `kind of body` EQUALS `a body corporate`
    AND facts's capacity EQUALS `a member of a body corporate whose affairs are managed by its members, in connection with the member’s functions of management`

-- Whether the kind of body is within paragraph (1) (F7.6).
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 37(1) and (2) (src:728-732)
GIVEN body IS A `Kind of body that offended under Article 37`
      interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 21 to 25, 37 to 43 and the endnotes`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the body is within Article 37(1)` body interp MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `the bodies within Article 37(1)`
    WHEN `only partnerships and bodies corporate` THEN
        CONSIDER body
        WHEN `a limited liability partnership` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `a separate limited partnership` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `a body corporate` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `an unincorporated association` THEN FALSE
        WHEN `the trustees of a trust` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `also unincorporated associations and trustees` THEN TRUE

-- Whether the capacity fits the kind of body (F7.10).  As printed: “a partner of the partnership” belongs with a limited liability partnership or a separate limited partnership, and “director, governor, secretary or other
-- similar officer of the body corporate” (and, by Article 37(2), the member of a body managed by its members) with a body corporate; for an unincorporated association or trustees (F7.6, other reading) the officer capacities are
-- taken as those of its governors.  The other reading takes any listed capacity for any body.
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 37(1) and (2) (src:728-732)
GIVEN body IS A `Kind of body that offended under Article 37`
      capacity IS A `Capacity of a person in relation to a body under Article 37`
      interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 21 to 25, 37 to 43 and the endnotes`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the capacity fits the body` body capacity interp MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `how capacity is paired with the kind of body in Article 37(1)`
    WHEN `any listed capacity for any body` THEN NOT (capacity EQUALS `none of these`)
    WHEN `capacity as printed for each kind of body` THEN
        CONSIDER capacity
        WHEN `a partner of the partnership` THEN body EQUALS `a limited liability partnership` OR body EQUALS `a separate limited partnership`
        WHEN `a director of the body corporate` THEN `the body has officers` body
        WHEN `a governor of the body corporate` THEN `the body has officers` body
        WHEN `a secretary of the body corporate` THEN `the body has officers` body
        WHEN `another officer similar to a director, governor or secretary of the body corporate` THEN `the body has officers` body
        WHEN `a member of a body corporate whose affairs are managed by its members, in connection with the member’s functions of management` THEN body EQUALS `a body corporate`
        WHEN `none of these` THEN FALSE

-- The kinds of body whose people are “directors, governors, secretaries or other similar officers”: a body corporate, and (on the other reading of F7.6) an unincorporated association or the trustees of a trust.
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 37(1) and (2) (src:728-732)
GIVEN body IS A `Kind of body that offended under Article 37`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the body has officers` body MEANS
    CONSIDER body
    WHEN `a limited liability partnership` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a separate limited partnership` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a body corporate` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an unincorporated association` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the trustees of a trust` THEN TRUE

-- 37(1) If an offence under this Law, committed by a limited liability partnership, a separate limited partnership or a body corporate is proved to have been committed with the consent or connivance of (a) a person who
-- is a partner of the partnership, or director, governor, secretary or other similar officer of the body corporate; or (b) any person purporting to act in any such capacity, the person is also guilty of the offence and liable in
-- the same manner as the partnership or body corporate to the penalty provided for that offence.
-- src:728 | (1) If an offence under this Law, committed by a limited liability partnership, a separate limited partnership or a body corporate is proved to have been committed with the consent or connivance of –
-- src:729 | (a) a person who is a partner of the partnership, or director, governor, secretary or other similar officer of the body corporate; or
-- src:730 | (b) any person purporting to act in any such capacity,
-- src:731 | the person is also guilty of the offence and liable in the same manner as the partnership or body corporate to the penalty provided for that offence.
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 37(1) (src:728-731)
@export Whether a person is also guilty, under Article 37(1) of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, of an offence committed by a limited liability partnership, a separate limited partnership or a body corporate: it is proved that the offence was committed with the person’s consent or connivance and the person is a partner, director, governor, secretary or similar officer of the body, or purports to act as one
GIVEN facts IS A `Facts for Article 37` @desc The facts Article 37 asks about
      interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 21 to 25, 37 to 43 and the endnotes` @desc The reading chosen at each fork; this function reads F7.6 and F7.10
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 37(1) the person is also guilty of the offence` facts interp MEANS
        `the body is within Article 37(1)` (facts's `kind of body`) interp
    AND facts's `the body committed an offence under this Law`
    AND facts's `consent or connivance is proved`
    AND `the capacity fits the body` (facts's `kind of body`) (facts's capacity) interp

@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 37(1) (src:731)
@export The penalty to which a person who is also guilty under Article 37(1) of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014 is liable: the penalty provided for the offence, in the same manner as the body; NOTHING where the person is not also guilty
GIVEN facts IS A `Facts for Article 37` @desc The facts Article 37 asks about
      `the penalty provided for the offence` IS A `Penalty provided` @desc The penalty the Law provides for the offence the body committed (integration point IP30)
      interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 21 to 25, 37 to 43 and the endnotes` @desc The reading chosen at each fork; this function reads F7.6 and F7.10
GIVETH A MAYBE `Penalty provided`
`Art 37(1) the penalty of the person who is also guilty` facts `the penalty provided for the offence` interp MEANS
    IF   `Art 37(1) the person is also guilty of the offence` facts interp
    THEN JUST `the penalty provided for the offence`
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:733 | 38 Service of notices
§§ `Article 38 — Service of notices`

-- 38 The Minister may by Order make further provision as to the service of any notice under this Law.  The Law itself does not say how the date of service is fixed (it is read in Articles 8(3)(k), 17(4), 26, 27, 33(3)).
-- src:734 | The Minister may by Order make further provision as to the service of any notice under this Law.
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 38 (src:734)
@export The power of the Minister by Order to make further provision as to the service of any notice under the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014 (Article 38), as a rule: the Minister may; the Order is an input
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Power exercised under Articles 38 to 42`
`Art 38 the Minister may make further provision as to service` MEANS
    PARTY `the Minister`
    MAY `make an Order making further provision as to the service of a notice`

-- F7.7.  The date on which a notice is served.  The Law fixes nothing but the Minister’s power; until an Order says otherwise the date of service is read as the date the notice is RECEIVED by the person served
-- (the reading taken) or the date it is SENT.  If an Order under Article 38 fixes a date (for example a number of days after posting), that date is an input and governs.
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 38 (src:734)
@export The date on which a notice under the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014 is served: the date an Order under Article 38 fixes, if there is one; otherwise, by the reading chosen at F7.7, the date the notice is received (default) or the date it is sent; a refusal where the reading needs a date of receipt and the notice has not been received
GIVEN `the date on which the notice was sent` IS A DATE @desc The day the notice was sent
      `the date on which the notice was received` IS A MAYBE DATE @desc The day the person served received it; NOTHING if it has not been received
      `the date fixed by an Order under Article 38` IS A MAYBE DATE @desc The date of service that an Order under Article 38 fixes for this notice, if any (an input: no Order is among the sources)
      interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 21 to 25, 37 to 43 and the endnotes` @desc The reading chosen at each fork; this function reads F7.7
GIVETH A DATE
`Art 38 the date on which the notice is served` `the date on which the notice was sent` `the date on which the notice was received` `the date fixed by an Order under Article 38` interp MEANS
    CONSIDER `the date fixed by an Order under Article 38`
    WHEN JUST fixed THEN fixed
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        CONSIDER interp's `the date on which a notice is served`
        WHEN `the date of sending` THEN `the date on which the notice was sent`
        WHEN `the date of receipt` THEN
            CONSIDER `the date on which the notice was received`
            WHEN JUST received THEN received
            WHEN NOTHING THEN `the notice has not been received, so on the reading taken it has not been served`

@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 38 (src:734)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`the notice has not been received, so on the reading taken it has not been served` MEANS
    REFUSE "the notice has not been received, so on the reading taken at F7.7 (the date of service is the date of receipt) it has not been served"

-- src:735 | 39 Regulations and Orders
§§ `Article 39 — Regulations and Orders`

-- The kinds of provision an Order or Regulations may contain besides their main content.
DECLARE `Kind of ancillary provision under Article 39` IS ONE OF
    `transitional`
    `consequential`
    `incidental`
    `supplementary`
    `savings`
    `an amendment of this Law`

-- 39(1) An Order or Regulations under this Law may contain such transitional, consequential, incidental, supplementary or savings provisions, other than an amendment of this Law, as appear to the Minister or the States
-- (as the case may be) to be necessary or expedient for the purposes of the Order or Regulations.  Two powers (the Minister’s, for an Order; the States’, for Regulations) and one limit (no amendment of this Law by this route:
-- the power to amend this Law by Order is Article 2(2), and by Regulations the powers named in 39(2)).  That it “appears to be necessary or expedient” is a discretion and an input.
-- src:736 | (1) An Order or Regulations under this Law may contain such transitional, consequential, incidental, supplementary or savings provisions, other than an amendment of this Law, as appear to the Minister or the States (as the case may be) to be necessary or expedient for the purposes of the Order or Regulations.
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 39(1) (src:736)
@export Whether a kind of ancillary provision may be contained in an Order or Regulations under Article 39(1) of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014: transitional, consequential, incidental, supplementary or savings provision, other than an amendment of the Law, that appears to the Minister or the States to be necessary or expedient
GIVEN kind IS A `Kind of ancillary provision under Article 39` @desc The kind of provision
      `it appears to be necessary or expedient for the purposes of the Order or Regulations` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The opinion of the Minister (for an Order) or of the States (for Regulations); a discretion the caller supplies
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 39(1) the provision may be contained in the Order or Regulations` kind `it appears to be necessary or expedient for the purposes of the Order or Regulations` MEANS
        `it appears to be necessary or expedient for the purposes of the Order or Regulations`
    AND (CONSIDER kind
         WHEN `transitional` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `consequential` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `incidental` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `supplementary` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `savings` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `an amendment of this Law` THEN FALSE)

@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 39(1) (src:736)
GIVEN `it appears to the Minister to be necessary or expedient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The Minister’s opinion (a discretion the caller supplies)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Power exercised under Articles 38 to 42`
`Art 39(1) the Minister may include ancillary provisions in an Order` `it appears to the Minister to be necessary or expedient` MEANS
    IF   `it appears to the Minister to be necessary or expedient`
    THEN PARTY `the Minister`
         MAY `include transitional, consequential, incidental, supplementary or savings provisions in an Order`
    ELSE FULFILLED

@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 39(1) (src:736)
GIVEN `it appears to the States to be necessary or expedient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The opinion of the States (a discretion the caller supplies)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Power exercised under Articles 38 to 42`
`Art 39(1) the States may include ancillary provisions in Regulations` `it appears to the States to be necessary or expedient` MEANS
    IF   `it appears to the States to be necessary or expedient`
    THEN PARTY `the States`
         MAY `include transitional, consequential, incidental, supplementary or savings provisions in Regulations`
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- 39(2) A power under this Law to amend, by Regulations, any provision of this Law includes the power to make such transitional, consequential, incidental or supplementary amendments to any other provision of this Law
-- as appears to the States to be necessary or expedient.
-- src:737 | (2) A power under this Law to amend, by Regulations, any provision of this Law includes the power to make such transitional, consequential, incidental or supplementary amendments to any other provision of this Law as appears to the States to be necessary or expedient.
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 39(2) (src:737)
@export The power of the States, when amending a provision of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014 by Regulations, to make the transitional, consequential, incidental or supplementary amendments of its other provisions that appear to the States to be necessary or expedient (Article 39(2)), as a rule
GIVEN `it appears to the States to be necessary or expedient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The opinion of the States (a discretion the caller supplies)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Power exercised under Articles 38 to 42`
`Art 39(2) the States may make consequential amendments of other provisions of the Law` `it appears to the States to be necessary or expedient` MEANS
    IF   `it appears to the States to be necessary or expedient`
    THEN PARTY `the States`
         MAY `make Regulations whose amendment of a provision of this Law includes consequential amendments of other provisions of it`
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- F7.14.  Article 2(2) lets the Minister by Order add sub-paragraphs to Article 2(1); Article 39(1) lets an Order contain consequential provisions “other than an amendment of this Law”; Article 39(2) extends a power to
-- amend this Law “by Regulations” to consequential amendments of its other provisions.  Reading taken: an Order under Article 2(2) cannot amend other provisions of the Law consequentially (R&O.56/2025 added
-- paragraph (ba) and changed nothing else, so Articles 2(3)(a), 2(5) and 8(3)(a)(ii) do not mention it).  The other reading: the power to add sub-paragraphs carries the consequential amendments.
-- src:66 | (2) The Minister may by Order add sub-paragraphs to paragraph (1).
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 2(2), Article 39(1) and (2) (src:66), (src:736-737)
@export Whether an Order under Article 2(2) of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014 may make a consequential amendment of another provision of the Law, on the reading chosen at F7.14 (default: no, Article 39(1) excludes an amendment of the Law from an Order and Article 39(2) speaks of Regulations)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 21 to 25, 37 to 43 and the endnotes` @desc The reading chosen at each fork; this function reads F7.14
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 39 an Order under Article 2(2) may make a consequential amendment of another provision of the Law` interp MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `whether an Order under Article 2(2) can make consequential amendments of other provisions of the Law`
    WHEN `only Regulations can make consequential amendments of this Law` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `an Order under Article 2(2) can make them too` THEN TRUE

-- src:738 | 40 Savings and transitional provisions
§§ `Article 40 — Savings and transitional provisions`

-- 40(1) Nothing in this Law is to be read as derogating from any power or function of the Attorney General, the Bailiff, or the court, being a power or function that exists independently of this Law (under customary law
-- or otherwise), in respect of charities or of acts for charitable purposes (whether as defined in this Law or otherwise).  INERT: a rule of construction.  It gives no input and decides no outcome of its own; it is carried as text.
-- src:739 | (1) Nothing in this Law is to be read as derogating from any power or function of the Attorney General, the Bailiff, or the court, being a power or function that exists independently of this Law (under customary law or otherwise), in respect of charities or of acts for charitable purposes (whether as defined in this Law or otherwise).
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 40(1) (src:739)
GIVETH A STRING
`Art 40(1) a rule of construction preserving the independent powers of the Attorney General, the Bailiff and the court` MEANS
    "Nothing in this Law is to be read as derogating from any power or function of the Attorney General, the Bailiff, or the court, being a power or function that exists independently of this Law (under customary law or otherwise), in respect of charities or of acts for charitable purposes (whether as defined in this Law or otherwise)."

-- 40(2) The States may by Regulations make such other saving provision as appears to the States to be necessary or expedient for the purposes of this Law.
-- src:740 | (2) The States may by Regulations make such other saving provision as appears to the States to be necessary or expedient for the purposes of this Law.
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 40(2) (src:740)
@export The power of the States by Regulations to make such other saving provision as appears to the States to be necessary or expedient for the purposes of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014 (Article 40(2)), as a rule
GIVEN `it appears to the States to be necessary or expedient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The opinion of the States (a discretion the caller supplies)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Power exercised under Articles 38 to 42`
`Art 40(2) the States may make other saving provision` `it appears to the States to be necessary or expedient` MEANS
    IF   `it appears to the States to be necessary or expedient`
    THEN PARTY `the States`
         MAY `make Regulations making other saving provision`
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- 40(3) The States may by Regulations make such transitional provision as appears to the States to be necessary or expedient for the purposes of the bringing into force of this Law.  The Law came wholly into force on
-- 1 January 2019 (g7-commencement); whether a power “for the purposes of the bringing into force” can still be exercised after that is not answered by the sources, and the rule does not test the day.
-- src:741 | (3) The States may by Regulations make such transitional provision as appears to the States to be necessary or expedient for the purposes of the bringing into force of this Law.
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 40(3) (src:741)
@export The power of the States by Regulations to make such transitional provision as appears to the States to be necessary or expedient for the purposes of the bringing into force of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014 (Article 40(3)), as a rule
GIVEN `it appears to the States to be necessary or expedient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The opinion of the States (a discretion the caller supplies)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Power exercised under Articles 38 to 42`
`Art 40(3) the States may make transitional provision for the bringing into force of the Law` `it appears to the States to be necessary or expedient` MEANS
    IF   `it appears to the States to be necessary or expedient`
    THEN PARTY `the States`
         MAY `make Regulations making transitional provision for bringing this Law into force`
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- src:742 | 41 Amendments of Laws relating to sharing of information
§§ `Article 41 — Amendments of Laws relating to sharing of information`

-- The kinds of enactment that Article 41(1) names; every other enactment is outside the power.
DECLARE `Kind of enactment under Article 41` IS ONE OF
    `the Proceeds of Crime (Supervisory Bodies) (Jersey) Law 2008`
    `an enactment under which the JFSC has functions`
    `an enactment relating to taxation`
    `some other enactment`

-- 41(1) For the purpose of paragraph (2) a relevant enactment is (a) the Proceeds of Crime (Supervisory Bodies) (Jersey) Law 2008; (b) any other enactment under which the JFSC has functions; (c) any enactment relating to taxation.
-- src:743 | (1) For the purpose of paragraph (2) a relevant enactment is –
-- src:744 | (a) the Proceeds of Crime (Supervisory Bodies) (Jersey) Law 2008;
-- src:745 | (b) any other enactment under which the JFSC has functions;
-- src:746 | (c) any enactment relating to taxation.
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 41(1) (src:743-746)
@export Whether an enactment is a relevant enactment under Article 41(1) of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014: the Proceeds of Crime (Supervisory Bodies) (Jersey) Law 2008, another enactment under which the JFSC has functions, or an enactment relating to taxation
GIVEN enactment IS A `Kind of enactment under Article 41` @desc The kind of enactment (whether an enactment is one of these is an input: the other enactments are not in the sources)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 41(1) the enactment is a relevant enactment` enactment MEANS
    CONSIDER enactment
    WHEN `the Proceeds of Crime (Supervisory Bodies) (Jersey) Law 2008` THEN "(a)" ... TRUE
    WHEN `an enactment under which the JFSC has functions` THEN "(b)" ... TRUE
    WHEN `an enactment relating to taxation` THEN "(c)" ... TRUE
    WHEN `some other enactment` THEN FALSE

-- 41(2) The States may by Regulations amend any relevant enactment, to make such provision as the States consider necessary or expedient to enable any supervisory body, the JFSC and the Comptroller to disclose information to
-- the Commissioner for the purposes of this Law.  A power of the States over OTHER enactments: encoded as a permission (ruling 4); the enactments and the Regulations are not in the sources.
-- src:747 | (2) The States may by Regulations amend any relevant enactment, to make such provision as the States consider necessary or expedient to enable any supervisory body, the JFSC and the Comptroller to disclose information to the Commissioner for the purposes of this Law.
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 41(2) (src:747)
@export The power of the States by Regulations to amend a relevant enactment to enable any supervisory body, the JFSC and the Comptroller to disclose information to the Commissioner (Article 41(2) of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014), as a rule: the States may, for a relevant enactment, if they consider it necessary or expedient
GIVEN enactment IS A `Kind of enactment under Article 41` @desc The enactment the Regulations would amend
      `the States consider the provision necessary or expedient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The opinion of the States (a discretion the caller supplies)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Power exercised under Articles 38 to 42`
`Art 41(2) the States may amend a relevant enactment to enable disclosure of information to the Commissioner` enactment `the States consider the provision necessary or expedient` MEANS
    IF   `Art 41(1) the enactment is a relevant enactment` enactment AND `the States consider the provision necessary or expedient`
    THEN PARTY `the States`
         MAY `make Regulations amending a relevant enactment to enable disclosure of information to the Commissioner`
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- src:748 | 42 Consequential amendments of other enactments
§§ `Article 42 — Consequential amendments of other enactments`

-- 42 The States may by Regulations (a) amend the Trusts (Jersey) Law 1984 and the Foundations (Jersey) Law 2009, to make provision for the application under those Laws of the charity test under this Law (by reference the test
-- itself, or to whether a trust or foundation is registered under this Law, or otherwise), or of the definition of charitable purposes under this Law, and for related purposes; (b) amend any other enactment (not including this
-- Law) to make such consequential provision as the States consider necessary or expedient, in relation to a reference (direct or indirect) in that other enactment to charities or charitable purposes or related terms.
-- src:749 | The States may by Regulations –
-- src:750 | (a) amend the Trusts (Jersey) Law 1984 and the Foundations (Jersey) Law 2009, to make provision for the application under those Laws of the charity test under this Law (by reference the test itself, or to whether a trust or foundation is registered under this Law, or otherwise), or of the definition of charitable purposes under this Law, and for related purposes;
-- src:751 | (b) amend any other enactment (not including this Law) to make such consequential provision as the States consider necessary or expedient, in relation to a reference (direct or indirect) in that other enactment to charities or charitable purposes or related terms.
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 42(a) (src:750)
@export The power of the States by Regulations to amend the Trusts (Jersey) Law 1984 and the Foundations (Jersey) Law 2009 to apply the charity test or the definition of charitable purposes (Article 42(a) of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014), as a rule
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Power exercised under Articles 38 to 42`
`Art 42(a) the States may amend the Trusts Law and the Foundations Law` MEANS
    PARTY `the States`
    MAY `make Regulations amending the Trusts (Jersey) Law 1984 and the Foundations (Jersey) Law 2009 to apply the charity test or the definition of charitable purposes`

@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 42(b) (src:751)
@export The power of the States by Regulations to amend any other enactment, not the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014 itself, for the consequential provision they consider necessary or expedient on references to charities, charitable purposes or related terms (Article 42(b)), as a rule
GIVEN `the enactment is the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014 itself` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the enactment to be amended is this Law (Article 42(b) excludes it)
      `the States consider the provision necessary or expedient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc The opinion of the States (a discretion the caller supplies)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Power exercised under Articles 38 to 42`
`Art 42(b) the States may amend another enactment for consequential provision on references to charities` `the enactment is the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014 itself` `the States consider the provision necessary or expedient` MEANS
    IF   (NOT `the enactment is the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014 itself`) AND `the States consider the provision necessary or expedient`
    THEN PARTY `the States`
         MAY `make Regulations amending another enactment, not this Law, for consequential provision on references to charities`
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- src:752 | 43 Citation
§§ `Article 43 — Citation`

-- 43 This Law may be cited as the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014.  INERT: citation only (the original Article 44(1)).
-- src:753 | This Law may be cited as the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014.
@ref Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Article 43 (src:753)
GIVETH A STRING
`Art 43 the short title` MEANS "Charities (Jersey) Law 2014"
